196,"Why are the prompt templates in Tables 10, 11, and 12 structured with explicit role-based instructions and formatted placeholders like '{question}' or '{reference}'?",Vanilla_RAG,qwen,deepseek,Reasoning,medium,CONCEPT,1,DEFAULT,-1.0,0.2078,0.4211,0.2783,0,0.0,1.0,0.0,6.15,0.00092,"The prompt templates in Tables 10, 11, and 12 use explicit role-based instructions (e.g., “You are an excellent AI doctor”) and formatted placeholders (e.g., `{question}`, `{reference}`) to enable **runtime adaptability, modularity, and task-specific behavior**. Role-based instructions prime the LLM with domain-relevant expertise and behavioral expectations, while curly-bracket placeholders allow dynamic substitution of inputs—supporting reuse across diverse instances without hard-coding values. This design aligns with the paper’s emphasis on *LM- and task-adaptive prompts* that are more powerful and modular than static, provider-specific hardcoded prompts.",ok,"Preprint
icantly lengthy prompt templates, with averages of 1337 and 722 characters, respectively. While
expert-written prompts can be useful, we believe that LM- and task-adaptive prompts bootstrapped
automatically can offer far more power (and are far more modular) than hard-coding a prompt per
database provider inside the code base. The next appendix section contains a number of prompts
copied from related research papers and existing libraries.
C
SAMPLE LARGE PROMPTS
This section highlights a few popular existing frameworks that structure prompts with extensive
prompt engineering templates. The primary objective is to capture how many words and characters
are used for such large multi-line prompts defined for tasks or tools and present these example
prompts retrieved from open-sourced papers and repositories. The formatting of these example
prompts is adapted from Gao et al. (2023a).
