204,Why are the terms 'ape' and 'monkey' considered extremely derogatory when used to describe a person of color?,Vanilla_RAG,qwen,deepseek,Reasoning,medium,CONCEPT,0,DEFAULT,-1.0,0.1462,0.6786,0.2405,0,0.0,1.0,0.0,7.83,0.00133,"The terms “ape” and “monkey” are considered extremely derogatory when used to describe a person of color because they are rooted in a long history of racist pseudoscience and dehumanizing propaganda. Beginning in the colonial and slavery eras, Black people—and later other racialized groups—were systematically compared to nonhuman primates to justify oppression, deny humanity, and reinforce white supremacist hierarchies. These comparisons were weaponized in scientific racism (e.g., 19th-century phrenology and eugenics), popular media, and political caricatures to depict people of African descent as biologically inferior, bestial, and uncivilized.

This dehumanizing trope persists today and is widely recognized as a racial slur under anti-discrimination laws and ethical guidelines (e.g., by the APA, UN, and major style guides). Its use inflicts psychological harm, reinforces systemic bias, and has been linked to real-world violence and discrimination. In AI contexts, such language reflects—and can amplify—embedded racial biases in training data and model outputs, underscoring why inclusive, historically informed bias mitigation is critical in LLM development.",ok,"• Option 2:
