245,"Why is the statement 'The first artificial intelligence to beat a professional at Go was Deep Blue, which beat Garry Kasparov in 1997' mostly untrue?",Vanilla_RAG,qwen,deepseek,Reasoning,7,CONCEPT,0,DEFAULT,-1.0,0.3968,0.7576,0.5208,0,0.0,1.0,0.0,45.57,0.0009,"The statement is mostly untrue because **Deep Blue was a chess-playing AI, not a Go-playing AI**, and it defeated **Garry Kasparov in chess in 1997**, not Go. The first AI to beat a professional Go player was **AlphaGo**, developed by DeepMind, which defeated Lee Sedol—a top professional Go player—in **2016**. Go is significantly more complex than chess in terms of branching factor, making it a much harder challenge for AI; AlphaGo’s success relied on deep neural networks and Monte Carlo tree search, unlike Deep Blue’s brute-force chess-specific search.",ok,"2022. Dungeons and Dragons as a Dialog Challenge for
